WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.856
Every order at the print shop starts on a paper form.

2
00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:12.229
There's a section for the customer, a section for the delivery address, and a box for the number of copies.

3
00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:14.552
Each section offers only its own boxes.

4
00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:18.065
So nobody writes a phone number where the copies should go.

5
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:21.944
In Kotlin, we can write that form as code.

6
00:00:21.944 --> 00:00:25.079
Each section becomes a block in braces.

7
00:00:25.079 --> 00:00:32.477
Inside a block, you fill in boxes by name: name equals Corner Bakery, phone equals 555-0142.

8
00:00:32.477 --> 00:00:34.487
No form named, no prefix.

9
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.975
That works because each block is a lambda with a receiver.

10
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:41.810
A lambda is a small function written in place.

11
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.016
Its receiver is the object it runs inside: its this.

12
00:00:45.016 --> 00:00:48.775
Picture a clerk handed a form already clipped to a clipboard.

13
00:00:48.775 --> 00:00:53.460
She writes in the boxes without saying which form, because she's holding it.

14
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:57.017
Behind each section sits a builder class.

15
00:00:57.017 --> 00:00:59.408
ContactBuilder offers name and phone.

16
00:00:59.408 --> 00:01:04.709
The customer and the delivery address both use it, since they need the same boxes.

17
00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:10.073
Around them, OrderFormBuilder offers copies, and the customer and deliverTo blocks.

18
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.454
Now picture a tired Friday afternoon.

19
00:01:13.454 --> 00:01:18.287
Inside the customer block, someone types: deliverTo, name equals oops.

20
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:22.015
That line belongs outside, in the order's own section.

21
00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.119
Does it compile?

22
00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:24.224
Pause and guess.

23
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.384
It compiles.

24
00:01:28.384 --> 00:01:28.958
Well...

25
00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:31.665
that's not what anybody expected.

26
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:35.205
So here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

27
00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:37.693
The customer section has no deliverTo.

28
00:01:37.693 --> 00:01:40.377
Where on earth did the compiler find one?

29
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:44.970
Inside the customer block, two receivers are in scope.

30
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.538
The nearest one is the ContactBuilder for the customer.

31
00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:50.874
Around it sits the OrderFormBuilder.

32
00:01:50.874 --> 00:01:56.064
For a call that names no receiver, Kotlin tries each one in turn, nearest first.

33
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:56.961
deliverTo?

34
00:01:56.961 --> 00:01:58.906
The ContactBuilder has none.

35
00:01:58.906 --> 00:02:06.548
So the compiler, always eager to help, looks one level out, finds deliverTo on the order form, and calls that.

36
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:13.407
On paper, that's a form where any box missing from your section gets written into the section around it.

37
00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:15.358
Picture the clerk who does that.

38
00:02:15.358 --> 00:02:16.150
Very helpful.

39
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.040
Never to be trusted with a pen.

40
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.673
So the point is: a name your block lacks falls through to the outer builder.

41
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:26.999
The lesson's demo runs exactly this wrong move.

42
00:02:26.999 --> 00:02:36.595
The misplaced line builds a second, half filled delivery address, and the program stops with: IllegalArgumentException, phone required for oops.

43
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.033
And that exception is luck.

44
00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:42.845
On a form with no required phone number, nothing would stop it.

45
00:02:42.845 --> 00:02:44.720
The shop would deliver to oops.

46
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.624
The van driver is still looking for that street.

47
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.522
What we really want is the paper rule back.

48
00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:54.409
Inside a section, only that section's boxes.

49
00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:58.294
Kotlin has a tool for exactly that, called DslMarker.

50
00:02:58.294 --> 00:03:02.575
It's an annotation, a label in the source that tools can read.

51
00:03:02.575 --> 00:03:05.752
You put it on an annotation class of your own.

52
00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:07.479
Here it's called FormDsl.

53
00:03:07.479 --> 00:03:10.517
Then you put FormDsl on every builder class.

54
00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:17.832
From then on, inside a block, only the nearest marked receiver is implicit, which means usable without naming it.

55
00:03:17.832 --> 00:03:19.905
The outer builder is still there.

56
00:03:19.905 --> 00:03:22.167
You just can't reach it by accident.

57
00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:26.356
Same wrong move, with both builders marked.

58
00:03:26.356 --> 00:03:27.577
What happens now?

59
00:03:27.577 --> 00:03:28.727
Pause and guess.

60
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.147
It doesn't compile.

61
00:03:33.147 --> 00:03:39.709
The compiler says the deliverTo function cannot be called in this context with an implicit receiver.

62
00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:42.203
Use an explicit receiver if necessary.

63
00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:45.509
A box from the outer section fails the same way.

64
00:03:45.509 --> 00:03:53.903
Write copies equals 5 inside the customer block, and the compiler says the copies property cannot be called in this context either.

65
00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:57.948
You can still reach the order on purpose, by naming it.

66
00:03:57.948 --> 00:04:00.237
this@orderForm is a labeled this.

67
00:04:00.237 --> 00:04:04.121
It names the receiver of the lambda passed to orderForm.

68
00:04:04.121 --> 00:04:09.046
On the paper form, that's writing: order section, copies, 250, in full.

69
00:04:09.046 --> 00:04:11.127
Nobody types that by accident.

70
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.060
Java never had this problem.

71
00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.444
A Java lambda gets the builder as a parameter with a name, c or f.

72
00:04:18.444 --> 00:04:24.423
So reaching the outer builder means typing f dot, on purpose, where a reviewer can see it.

73
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.832
Now the rules, because they're narrow.

74
00:04:27.832 --> 00:04:29.674
One: mark every builder class.

75
00:04:29.674 --> 00:04:34.523
For a language with twenty builders, marking their common superclass is enough.

76
00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:38.427
Two: the marker counts only where it marks a type.

77
00:04:38.427 --> 00:04:44.411
On a function it does nothing, and Kotlin 2.4.21 warns you that it has no effect.

78
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.519
And here the paper picture breaks, in our favor.

79
00:04:48.519 --> 00:04:52.104
A clerk finds a misfiled box after the form is handed in.

80
00:04:52.104 --> 00:04:55.312
The compiler finds it before the program ever runs.

81
00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.686
So, where did deliverTo come from?

82
00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:03.795
From the outer builder, because a missing name falls through, nearest first.

83
00:05:03.795 --> 00:05:08.165
DslMarker stops the fall: each section offers only its own boxes.

84
00:05:08.165 --> 00:05:09.845
Mark every builder class.

85
00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:10.944
Quick check.

86
00:05:10.944 --> 00:05:15.986
You take FormDsl off ContactBuilder, but keep it on OrderFormBuilder.

87
00:05:15.986 --> 00:05:17.959
Does the oops line compile?

88
00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:25.221
It compiles again, and the shop is back to delivering to oops.

89
00:05:25.221 --> 00:05:27.990
The nearest receiver needs the marker too.

90
00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.111
So mark them all.
